  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## **\\-Ed/-ing** adjectives\n\n![Color-coded chart demonstrating how to form -ed and -ing adjectives from verbs, with three columns showing the base verb, the adjective that describes the feeling it inspires, and the adjective describing how someone feels](/uploads/grammar/b1/ed-ing-adjectives-adjectives-verbs/img-1.png)\n\nA lot of [adjectives](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/adjectives/) are made from verbs by adding **\\-ing** or **\\-ed**. They are called participial adjectives they take the forms of the present (**\\-ing**) and past participle (**\\-ed**) of the verb.\n\n#### **\\-Ed** adjectives\n\n**\\-ed** adjectives are used to describe **how we feel**. Look at the following examples:\n\n-   *We’re **tired**. Can we stop running?*\n-   *I’m **bored**. Let’s play cards.*\n-   *I was **surprised** to see her.*\n\n#### **\\-Ing** adjectives\n\n**\\-ing** adjectives are used to talk about the **things that produce these feelings**. Check the examples below:\n\n-   *Running is very **tiring**.* *We don’t want to run.*\n-   *This film is very **boring**. Let’s play cards.*\n-   *Her visit was very **surprising**. We weren’t expecting her.*",
